---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import PercentageCalculator from '@/components/tools/math/PercentageCalculator.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="percentage-calculator">
  <PercentageCalculator client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to use the percentage calculator</h2>
    <p>
      Percentage questions come in four shapes, and mixing them up is the single most common source of
      arithmetic mistakes. Pick the tab that matches the sentence you are trying to answer:
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <strong>X% of Y</strong>: “what is 15% of 240?”. Use this for tips, sales tax, commission and any
        share of a known total.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>X is what % of Y</strong>: “36 out of 240 is what percent?”. Use this for test scores,
        conversion rates and market share.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>% increase or decrease</strong>: “sales went from 80 to 100, by what percent?”. Use this
        to compare a new figure with an earlier one.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>X plus or minus Y%</strong>: “add 20% VAT to 250” or “take 30% off 89.99”. Use this to
        apply a percentage, not to measure one.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Every tab updates as you type and shows the full working underneath, including the decimal multiplier
      it used. Copy the steps with one button if you need to paste them into homework or a spreadsheet note.
    </p>

    <h2>The four formulas</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Question</th>
            <th scope="col">Formula</th>
            <th scope="col">Worked example</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>What is X% of Y?</td>
            <td>(X ÷ 100) × Y</td>
            <td>15% of 240 = 0.15 × 240 = 36</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>X is what percent of Y?</td>
            <td>(X ÷ Y) × 100</td>
            <td>36 of 240 = 0.15 × 100 = 15%</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Percentage change old → new</td>
            <td>(new − old) ÷ |old| × 100</td>
            <td>80 → 100 = 20 ÷ 80 = +25%</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Add or subtract a percentage</td>
            <td>X × (1 ± Y ÷ 100)</td>
            <td>250 + 20% = 250 × 1.2 = 300</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Percentage traps worth knowing</h2>
    <h3>Percentages do not add up</h3>
    <p>
      A 10% rise followed by another 10% rise is not a 20% rise. The two multipliers compound:
      1.10 × 1.10 = 1.21, so the total is a 21% increase. The same logic explains why a 50% discount followed
      by a further 20% off is a 60% discount, not 70%: 0.5 × 0.8 = 0.4, and you pay 40% of the original.
    </p>
    <h3>Going up and back down does not return you to the start</h3>
    <p>
      Rise 25% from 80 and you get 100. Fall 25% from 100 and you get 75, not 80. The rise was measured
      against 80 and the fall against 100: different bases give different steps. To undo a p%
      increase, you need a decrease of p ÷ (100 + p) × 100 percent.
    </p>
    <h3>Percentage points are not percent</h3>
    <p>
      If an interest rate moves from 4% to 5%, that is a rise of one <em>percentage point</em> but a 25%
      increase in the rate itself. News reports mix these up constantly; the “% increase” tab always reports
      the relative change, which is the second reading.
    </p>
    <h3>Percentage change versus percentage difference</h3>
    <p>
      Percentage change is directional and uses the starting value as its base. Percentage difference is
      symmetric and uses the average of the two values, so comparing A with B gives the same answer as
      comparing B with A. The change tab shows both figures so you can quote the right one.
    </p>

    <h2>Quick mental shortcuts</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>For 10%, move the decimal point one place left. 10% of 63 is 6.3.</li>
      <li>5% is half of 10%. 5% of 63 is 3.15.</li>
      <li>For 1%, move the point two places left. 1% of 63 is 0.63.</li>
      <li>A 15% tip is 10% plus half of it again. 10% of 48 is 4.80, plus 2.40, gives 7.20.</li>
      <li>X% of Y always equals Y% of X. 18% of 50 is awkward, but 50% of 18 is clearly 9.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Accuracy and privacy</h2>
    <p>
      Calculations run in double-precision floating point, the same arithmetic every spreadsheet uses, and
      results are shown to up to ten decimal places with trailing zeros trimmed. Nothing you type is
      transmitted anywhere: the calculator is JavaScript that runs inside your browser tab, so it keeps
      working with the network switched off.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
